@/*
** ++
**
**  Facility:  SGL - Signaling library
**
**  Abstract:
**      This file will contain a library of routines for signalling conditions.
**
**  !!!!!  I've kludged around in here.  The problem is apparently that
**         stdout and stderr are pointer into the TID, so you can't cache
**         them in a static SGLhndl without screwing up other tasks.  Don't
**         know how to fix this yet, so for now output goes directly to
**         stdout and stderr without a logging capability.
**
**  Comments of May 17, 1994 after trying to fix this library yet again...
**    I'm not sure this library is really worth the effort to fix.  The only
**    place it's really used is in Vbb and I don't anticipate too many other
**    interactive I/O programs like it in the future.  So for the meantime,
**    I've forced SGL_Output to output to stdout and SGL_Error to logMsg.
**    SGL_Report remains the same.
**    I think I've come up with two solutions for the problem.  ("The problem"
**    is that I would like to be able to redirect stdout, stderr and a log
**    FILE descriptor on the fly, without having to pass a SGL handle to each
**    of the SGL routines.  logMsg sort of does what we want and is therefore
**    appropriate in the stderr case, but since it wants to prepend some stuff
**    to the output line, it is not appropriate for regular output.
**    One solution is to store a pointer to the task specific SGL handle in
**    TCB.  WRS gives us 4 spare locations to do this.  Problems with this are
**    useage of the TCB spare resource, no guarantee that WRS will let us do
**    this in the future, possibly no way to tell that SGL handle is uninited,
**    or maybe doesn't even exist, as in the case of a subroutine being called
**    from the shell.
**    A second solution is to keep a static doubly linked list header.  Then
**    every task that calls SGL_Init would add a link to the list.  Each link
**    would contain a pointer to the output, error and log FILE descriptors,
**    and the task ID.  SGL_Output and SGL_Error would then traverse the list
**    looking for the TID of the caller and if found use the output, error
**    and/or log FILE descriptors found therein.  If not found, they would use
**    stdout, stderr and NILL, respectively.  Problems with this are that it
**    takes CPU to traverse the list, worst case is when there is no entry for
**    the task (not terrible, but kinda gross), have to figure out how to deal
**    with the case where a task crashed and left an entry and a new, different
**    task comes up with the old TID.  SGL_Init could detect a stale handle with
**    an if (first) sort of mechanism, but SGL_Init has to be called for that.
**    Have to deal with SGL_Init being called multiple times from the same task.
**    Probably okay to just update the handle in these two (sort of the same)
**    cases.
**
**    OK, enough time wasted on this stuff for a while.
**
**    April 13, 1995 - I wanted to get messages back to EPICS, so I added the
**    call-back stuff.  Only one entry is allowed, and there is no protection
**    against multiple calls to SGL_Init.  If SGL_Init isn't called, the
**    SGL routines boil down to a simple logMsg.
**
**    Still need to add a delete function to change the effects of SGL_Init.
**
**    September 11, 1995 - More reworking.  Calling SGL_Init must be left as
**    optional because there are times that things are run from the shell that
**    shouldn't have to call to SGL_Init.  So there is now one static header
**    for all those types of situations.
**    The problem with leaving only one header is that if multiple tasks try
**    to signal at the same time, the message buffer would get overwritten.  So
**    to prevent this, we now allow for each task to call SGL_Init and get its
**    own handle which includes its own message buffer.  Doing that also
**    conveniently allowed each SGL_Init caller to be able to register its own
**    call-back and logging stream.
**    If a task is about to exit, it should free its signal handle by calling
**    SGL_Free.  The list of handles is kept in the static one.
**    So now my concern is that there may be some reenterancy problems.  Not
**    sure if this is real or not.
**
**  Author:
**	R. Claus, SLAC/PEP-II Feedback Project
**
**  History:
**	August     6, 1993 - Created.
**      March     22, 1994 - Bugs.  See kludge comment above.
**      May       17, 1994 - Made _Output and _Error jackets for printf and
**                           logMsg, respectively.  Made _Init a noop.
**      April     13, 1995 - Added call-back stuff
**      August    16, 1995 - Added timestamp and finding of function name
**      September 11, 1995 - Allowed for tasks to get private handles, _Free.
**      March     17, 1997 - Add string.h for strlen()
**
**  Copyright:
